\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Data and models.} We evaluate on Arena-Hard \citep{li2024arenahard}, a benchmark of 316 multi-turn conversational items selected for high separability among frontier models. After filtering for coverage, the evaluation set comprises 176 items in a connected incomplete design (36{,}231 observations; 66.0\% fill rate of the full $N \times I \times J$ tensor). Seventeen target models spanning proprietary and open-source families are evaluated: the original 8 models (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, Qwen2.5-72B, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Qwen3-8B, Gemma-2-9B, GPT-4-0613, GPT-3.5-Turbo) plus 9 additional models (Claude-3.5-Sonnet, GPT-4-Turbo, LLaMA-3.1-70B, Gemma-2-27B, Claude-3-Opus, LLaMA-3-70B, Mixtral-8x22B, Claude-3-Haiku, LLaMA-3-8B). Ground-truth rankings are derived from Chatbot Arena human preference data \citep{zheng2023mtbench}.

\paragraph{Judges.} Nine LLM judges score model responses in position-swapped pairwise mode: GPT-5.5, Claude Opus 4.7, Claude Sonnet 4.6, DeepSeek-V4-Pro, Gemini-2.5-Pro, Mistral-Large-2, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Qwen2.5-72B, and Qwen3-235B. Seven judges cover all 17 target models; two judges (GPT-5.5 and Claude Opus 4.7) cover only the original 8 models, yielding an asymmetric connected design---Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient} requires only connectivity, not full crossing. Position swapping controls for position bias and doubles the effective observation count. Two models---Qwen2.5-72B and LLaMA-3.3-70B---serve as both judge and target; $d_j$ absorbs systematic self-evaluation bias, and excluding self-judging observations leaves rankings unchanged ($\tau = 0.857$; Appendix~\ref{app:self_eval}).

\paragraph{Exposure estimation.} Per-item exposure $e_i$ is estimated via a two-source pipeline (\S\ref{sec:staged}): reference model anchoring with gpt-4-0314 as anchor (75\% weight) and cross-model agreement (25\% weight). The pipeline is robust to weight configuration (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.998$ across all configurations tested).

\paragraph{MCMC.} We use NumPyro's NUTS sampler with 4{,}000 warmup and 4{,}000 sampling iterations across 4 chains (target acceptance 0.95). The contamination prior follows the hierarchical HalfNormal specification (Eq.~\ref{eq:contam_prior}). Convergence is assessed via rank-normalized split-$\hat{R}$ \citep{vehtari2021rhat} with conventional $\hat{R} < 1.05$ and ESS $> 100$ thresholds \citep{gelman2013bda}.

\subsection{Baselines and Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

We compare G-MFRM against seven methods that address judge bias or contamination separately: (1) Raw Average, (2) MFRM-only (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}), (3) Sequential pipeline (MFRM then contamination adjustment), (4) CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, (5) CyclicJudge+discard (exposed items removed), (6) DCR Factor \citep{dcr2025}, and (7) Preference Leakage calibration \citep{preference_leakage2026}. All methods are evaluated by Kendall $\tau$ and Spearman $\rho$ against Chatbot Arena human rankings.

All seven baselines produce identical rankings ($\tau = 0.786$, $\rho = 0.905$; raw ability scores differ by $< 0.03$ logits, Table~\ref{tab:raw_scores}). G-MFRM achieves nominally higher rank agreement ($\tau = 0.857$, $\rho = 0.952$), but bootstrap $\Delta\tau$ 95\% CI $[-0.071, 0.071]$ includes zero at $N = 8$, though $P(\Delta\tau \geq 0) = 0.927$. On synthetic data ($N = 50$), the improvement is significant ($\tau = 0.922$ vs.\ $0.839$, Wilcoxon $p < 0.001$), confirming the effect is real but below detection at this sample size.

\paragraph{Predictive fit.} LOO cross-validation strongly favors G-MFRM ($\Delta\mathrm{ELPD}_{\mathrm{LOO}} = -55.7$, SE $= 9.4$, $|\Delta/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$; \citealp{vehtari2017loo}), with stacking weight 1.0 and all Pareto $\hat{k} < 0.7$ (Appendix~\ref{app:loo_comparison}).

\paragraph{Ranking accuracy.} The $\Delta\tau = +0.071$ improvement is not statistically significant at $N = 8$ (bootstrap 95\% CI crosses zero; post-hoc power ${\sim}$50\%). Power analysis indicates $N \approx 17$ for 80\% power (Appendix~\ref{app:power}), validated at $N = 20$ where the improvement reaches significance ($p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{app:synthetic_calibration}).

All parameters achieve $\hat{R} \leq 1.03$ with ESS $\geq 174$ and zero divergent transitions; model- and judge-level fit statistics fall within acceptable ranges (MNSQ $\in [0.63, 1.07]$; full diagnostics in Appendices~\ref{app:item_diagnostics} and~\ref{app:ppc}). Contamination susceptibility $\hat{c}_n$ exhibits a gradient by model age (Figure~\ref{fig:contamination_bars}): the four oldest models show $\hat{c}_n > 1.0$ (GPT-3.5-Turbo: $5.60$; GPT-4-0613: $4.20$; Qwen2.5-72B: $3.00$; LLaMA-3.3-70B: $2.50$), while recent models show low contamination (GPT-4o: $0.88$; GPT-4o-mini: $0.93$). MFRM-only inflates ability estimates for the most contaminated models by up to 0.79 logits (Figure~\ref{fig:bias_absorption}; Table~\ref{tab:bias_absorption}). Judge severity $d_j$ separates by provenance: proprietary judges score more strictly, spanning 1.34 logits (Figure~\ref{fig:judge_severity}; Table~\ref{tab:judge_severity}). Results are robust to prior specification and judge panel size ($k \geq 4$ suffices; Appendices~\ref{app:prior_robustness} and~\ref{app:judge_count}).

\subsection{Error Propagation and Ablation}
\label{sec:error_prop_results}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ablation_rmse}
\caption{Ablation on synthetic data ($N = 50$, 20 seeds). Joint estimation drives the improvement: staged estimation doubles $\theta$-RMSE ($p < 0.001$). Continuous gating provides a moderate gain over binary ($p < 0.001$); removing judge severity $d_j$ has negligible effect on ability recovery (n.s.).}
\label{fig:ablation}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:error_prop} validates the theoretical prediction (\S\ref{sec:error_prop}): below $\sigma = 0.2$, ability RMSE increases by 5.9\% and contamination RMSE by 7.9\%; beyond the threshold ($\sigma = 0.5$), degradation becomes substantial. Ablation (Figure~\ref{fig:ablation}; full results in Table~\ref{tab:ablation}, Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}) confirms joint estimation as the dominant contributor ($2.0\times$ $\theta$-RMSE increase under staged estimation, $p < 0.001$) and continuous gating over binary ($c_n$-RMSE $-$40\%, $p < 0.001$).

\paragraph{Judge panel composition.} Subsampling from the 9-judge panel (Table~\ref{tab:judge_count}, Appendix~\ref{app:judge_count}), G-MFRM's ranking accuracy increases monotonically with judge count ($\tau$: $0.771 \to 0.857$) and exceeds the full-panel baseline at $k \geq 4$. Leave-one-judge-out analysis identifies three judges spanning the severity extremes---Claude Opus 4.7 ($d_j = +0.60$), GPT-5.5 ($+0.53$), and LLaMA-3.3-70B ($-0.42$)---as individually necessary for the ranking improvement; removing any one reduces $\tau$ to the baseline $0.786$, while removing any other judge preserves $\tau = 0.857$. This suggests that severity diversity matters more than panel size for contamination--ability disentanglement, providing practical guidance for judge panel design.

